<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inter caetera</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1493">1493</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Inter caetera is a papal bull issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a> in 1493, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s first voyage. It granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> rights over lands discovered to the west of a line in the Atlantic, dividing spheres of exploration with Portugal. It provided a legal and religious basis for the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inter-caetera.jpg" alt="Inter caetera" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tzzzpfff at German Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S%C3%BCdamerika1650.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> had first approached King John II of Portugal, whose scholars judged that his calculations grossly underestimated the diameter of the Earth. On his return from the first voyage a storm drove his ship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> on 4 March 1493. John II believed the voyage broke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-alcacovas">Treaty of Alcáçovas</a> of 1479, ratified by the papal bull Aeterni regis of 1481, which he understood to give Portugal all territory south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>.</p><p>On 11 April the Spanish ambassador told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a>, a Spaniard, of the discoveries and urged a bull favourable to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The pope was then in a territorial dispute with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-naples">Ferdinand I of Naples</a>, a first cousin of the Spanish king, and was amicable to the requests of Isabella and Ferdinand. He issued edicts dated 3 and 4 May 1493.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The first bull, Inter caetera of 3 May, recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s claim to discovered lands not already held by a Christian prince and protected Portugal's earlier rights; both parties found it too vague. The second, Eximiae devotionis, gave the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León the privileges Portugal had in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>. The third, also called Inter caetera and dated 4 May, superseded the first two. It urged the Spanish monarchs to spread the faith and assigned them the lands to the west and south of a pole-to-pole line one hundred leagues west and south of any of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azores">Azores</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a> islands. The line divided only the Atlantic zones.</p><p>It remains unclear whether the pope meant a donation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> or an investiture. Some argue that it only turned possession and occupation into lawful sovereignty, while the Spanish crown and the conquistadors read it as giving full political sovereignty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>John II ignored the bulls, neither admitting their authority nor defying the Church, and pursued negotiations. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a> of 1494 moved the line to a meridian 370 leagues west of the Cape Verde Islands and gave Portugal all newly discovered lands east of it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s attempts to persuade other European powers of the bull's validity never succeeded, and within Spain Francisco de Vitoria denounced it.</p><p>Chief Justice John Marshall cited its authorisation to take the lands of non-Christian peoples almost 300 years later while developing the discovery doctrine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>. In the 21st century groups including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/native-hawaiians">Kanaka Maoli</a> organised protests and petitions seeking its repeal, citing a record of conquest, disease and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> that they say still harms their cultures.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Inter caetera” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire's legal and political foundations were set out in the papal bulls of 1493, above all <b>Inter caetera</b>, which assigned Spain the right to lands discovered to the west, and the Treaty of Tordesillas (1494), which fixed the meridian dividing the Spanish and Portuguese spheres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The bull <b>Inter caetera</b> drew a line west of the Azores, granting Spain lands beyond it.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-4">4 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
